---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { basePath, siteUrl } from '../lib/site';
import { ALL_TOOLS } from '../lib/tools';

const title = 'Image Tools - Free Online Image Utilities | GratisBench';
const description =
  'Free online image tools: compress images, convert formats, remove backgrounds with AI, read text from images (OCR), and trace images to SVG vectors. No signup, no watermark. Everything runs in your browser.';

const toolsRaw = [
  { href: '/image-compressor', name: 'Image compressor', desc: 'Shrink photos and screenshots with a quality slider, batch and ZIP included.' },
  { href: '/image-converter', name: 'Image converter', desc: 'Convert between PNG, JPEG, WebP, and AVIF, with a transparency warning for JPEG.' },
  { href: '/background-remover', name: 'Background remover', desc: 'AI background removal in your browser. Full-resolution transparent PNG, free.' },
  { href: '/image-ocr', name: 'Image OCR', desc: 'Read text from photos and scans with Tesseract. Copy or download .txt.' },
  { href: '/image-tracer', name: 'Image tracer', desc: 'Turn logos and icons into clean SVG vectors. Free SVG download.' },
] as const;

const tagByHref = new Map(ALL_TOOLS.map((t) => [t.href, t.tag]));
const tools = toolsRaw.map((t, i) => ({ ...t, num: String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0'), tag: tagByHref.get(t.href) ?? '' }));

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'GratisBench image tools',
  url: siteUrl(Astro.site) + basePath('/image-tools'),
  description,
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={basePath("/")}>GratisBench</a><span><span class="sep">/</span><a href={basePath("/image-tools")}>Image tools</a></span>
      </nav>
      <h1>Image tools</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Five utilities for everyday image jobs: shrinking files with a quality slider you control,
        converting between the formats your software and websites actually accept, removing
        backgrounds with AI, reading text out of photos, and tracing logos into SVG vectors.
        All free, all private, all in your browser.
      </p>
      <p class="privacy-line">Your files never leave your device.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Ad slot: hub top (AdSense display unit goes here) -->
    <div class="ad-slot" data-ad-slot="hub-top" aria-hidden="true"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

    <div class="tool-index">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <a class="tool-row" href={basePath(t.href)}>
          <span class="tool-row-num">{t.num}</span>
          <span>
            <span class="tool-row-name">{t.name}</span>
            <p class="tool-row-desc">{t.desc}</p>
          </span>
          <span class="tool-row-tag">{t.tag}</span>
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      <h2>What these tools do</h2>
      <p>
        Most image work is not editing. It is preparing: a photo that is too big to email, a WebP
        that your software cannot open, a BMP scan eating disk space for no reason. The compressor
        handles the first job with a quality slider and per-file before/after sizes, so you can see
        exactly what each setting costs. The converter handles the rest, redrawing each image in the
        format you pick.
      </p>
      <p>
        A useful pattern: run heavy photos through <a href={basePath("/image-compressor")}>Image compressor</a>
        first at quality 80, then use <a href={basePath("/image-converter")}>Image converter</a> only when the
        format itself is the problem, such as a PNG that needs to become a JPEG for an upload form. Both tools
        process batches and hand you a single ZIP at the end. Everything stays on your device the whole way through.
      </p>
      <p>
        The three newer tools go further. The <a href={basePath("/background-remover")}>background remover</a> runs
        an AI segmentation model in your browser and gives you a full-resolution transparent PNG, which paid
        services charge per image for. <a href={basePath("/image-ocr")}>Image OCR</a> reads text out of photos and
        scans with Tesseract, with copy and .txt download. And the <a href={basePath("/image-tracer")}>image
        tracer</a> turns logos and icons into clean SVG vectors with a free download. Same deal as the rest:
        free, private, in your browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limitations, stated up front</h2>
      <p>
        PNG is lossless, so compressing a PNG as PNG barely changes its size; real savings come from converting
        to JPEG or WebP, and both tools say so where it matters. JPEG has no transparency, so converting a
        transparent image to JPEG forces a background color: the tools warn you and offer a white fill instead of
        silently turning those areas black. AVIF output appears only when your browser can actually encode it.
        Animated GIFs convert as a single still frame.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Are these image tools free?</dt>
          <dd>Yes. All five tools are free with no limits, no watermarks, and no account.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Do my images get uploaded?</dt>
          <dd>No. All five tools process your files locally in the browser. Nothing is sent to a server, which is why they also work offline once loaded. The background remover and OCR download their AI models once, then work offline too.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Will my images lose quality?</dt>
          <dd>It depends on the settings. JPEG, WebP, and AVIF outputs are lossy and discard some detail; PNG output is lossless. The compressor shows the size saving per file so you can judge the trade-off, and nothing is final until you download.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>What about transparency?</dt>
          <dd>PNG, WebP, and AVIF keep the alpha channel. JPEG cannot store transparency, so both tools warn you when an image actually has transparent pixels and offer a white background fill for JPEG output.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <!-- Ad slot: hub bottom (AdSense display unit goes here) -->
    <div class="ad-slot" data-ad-slot="hub-bottom" aria-hidden="true"><span>Advertisement</span></div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
